{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":67428385,"ns":0,"title":"Defeasibility (linguistics)","extract":"In the linguistic field of pragmatics, an inference is said to be defeasible or cancellable if it can be made to disappear by the addition of another statement, or an appropriate context. For example, sentence [i] would normally implicate [ii] by scalar implicature:\n\ni: Alice has three children.","description":"Term in linguistics","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}